{"case_id": "uwf_eva_visible_0028", "dataset": "uwf", "arquitectura": "nodex_multiagente", "repeticion": 1, "config_version": "enriquecido-12casos-r1-20261104", "run_id": "uwf_eva_visible_0028__rag30__r1__9f931468", "condicion_rag": "enriquecido", "rag_version": "rag_enriquecido_30_v1", "casos_historicos": 30, "event": "memory_query", "event_index": 8, "utc": "2026-11-04T23:07:26.612579+00:00", "query_id": "rag-query-6", "collection": "attack_techniques", "query_text": "Lote de tipo zeek. Predominan eventos de evento_critico sobre el servicio dns (270 eventos). 270 eventos criticos del sistema registrados. Actividad de 16 IPs distintas.", "returned_documents": 3, "candidates": [{"vector_id": "attack-T1584.002", "rank": 1, "distance": 0.5486266613006592, "similarity": 0.4513733386993408, "returned_by_memory": true, "text": "DNS Server. Adversaries may compromise third-party DNS servers that can be used during targeting. During post-compromise activity, adversaries may utilize DNS traffic for various tasks, including for Command and Control (ex: [Application Layer Protocol](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1071)). Instead of setting up their own DNS servers, adversaries may compromise third-party DNS servers in support of operations.\n\nBy compromising DNS servers, adversaries can alter DNS records. Such control can allow for redirection of an organization's traffic, facilitating Collection and Credential Access efforts for the adversary.(Citation: Talos DNSpionage Nov 2018)(Citation: FireEye DNS Hijack 2019)  Additionally, adversaries may leverage such control in conjunction with [Digital Certificates](http", "metadata": {"url": "https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1584/002", "technique_id": "T1584.002", "tactics": "resource-development", "name": "DNS Server", "is_subtechnique": true}, "knowledge_type": "mitre_static"}, {"vector_id": "attack-T1071.004", "rank": 2, "distance": 0.5512709021568298, "similarity": 0.44872909784317017, "returned_by_memory": true, "text": "DNS. Adversaries may communicate using the Domain Name System (DNS) application layer protocol to avoid detection/network filtering by blending in with existing traffic. Commands to the remote system, and often the results of those commands, will be embedded within the protocol traffic between the client and server. \n\nThe DNS protocol serves an administrative function in computer networking and thus may be very common in environments. DNS traffic may also be allowed even before network authentication is completed. DNS packets contain many fields and headers in which data can be concealed. Often known as DNS tunneling, adversaries may abuse DNS to communicate with systems under their control within a victim network while also mimicking normal, expected traffic.(Citation: PAN DNS Tunneling)(", "metadata": {"url": "https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1071/004", "technique_id": "T1071.004", "is_subtechnique": true, "tactics": "command-and-control", "name": "DNS"}, "knowledge_type": "mitre_static"}, {"vector_id": "attack-T1590.002", "rank": 3, "distance": 0.5653384327888489, "similarity": 0.4346615672111511, "returned_by_memory": true, "text": "DNS. Adversaries may gather information about the victim's DNS that can be used during targeting. DNS information may include a variety of details, including registered name servers as well as records that outline addressing for a target’s subdomains, mail servers, and other hosts. DNS MX, TXT, and SPF records may also reveal the use of third party cloud and SaaS providers, such as Office 365, G Suite, Salesforce, or Zendesk.(Citation: Sean Metcalf Twitter DNS Records)\n\nAdversaries may gather this information in various ways, such as querying or otherwise collecting details via [DNS/Passive DNS](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1596/001). DNS information may also be exposed to adversaries via online or other accessible data sets (ex: [Search Open Technical Databases](https://attack.mit", "metadata": {"tactics": "reconnaissance", "is_subtechnique": true, "name": "DNS", "technique_id": "T1590.002", "url": "https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1590/002"}, "knowledge_type": "mitre_static"}], "ties_among_top_k": false, "ties_scope": "solo candidatos observados dentro del top-k"}
